{# Shown until the question has an answer, and then never again.

   One line, and it tells rather than asks. The cookies that keep a search and a
   booking together cannot be declined -- the site stops working without them --
   so offering them as a choice would be a question with one possible answer.
   They are stated. Analytics is the only part that is actually optional, and
   the two buttons are where that is offered.

   The first version explained both vendors, named Webvisor and listed which
   cookies were exempt: fifty words and a slab two hundred pixels tall on a
   phone. None of it stopped being true, it moved to /cookies, where somebody
   who wants it is already looking.

   Not a modal, and it does not trap focus: nothing on this site is withheld
   until you decide, and a bar that blocks the page to ask about analytics is
   worse than the analytics.

   The data attributes are how global.js learns the cookie's name, its two
   values and its lifetime, so src/Consent.php stays the only place they are
   spelled. #}
<section class="cookie-notice js-cookie-notice"
         aria-label="Cookies"
         data-cookie="{{ consent.cookie }}"
         data-max-age="{{ consent.max_age }}">
    {# The band runs edge to edge; what is written on it does not. Inside the
       same .container every other row of this site uses, so the question lines
       up with the footer headings directly above it rather than sitting 58px
       further out on its own margin. #}
    <div class="container cookie-notice__inner">
        {# Drawn here rather than set in Font Awesome, which has no cookie in
           colour -- and a flat two-tone one reads at this size where a
           monochrome glyph just reads as a dot. Inline for the reason the
           breadcrumb separator and the favicon are: no second request for
           twenty lines of shapes, and nothing to go missing on a deploy.

           The body is r=10.4 in a 24 box on purpose. At r=9.5 it filled 19 of
           its 24 units, so a box sized to the buttons drew a cookie a third
           smaller than them -- the box matched and the drawing did not.

           Its own column beside the text, not an <i> inside it. Inline, the
           icon belonged to the first line and the second and third started
           underneath it; here the row is icon-then-block, so it stays at the
           left however many lines the sentence takes. #}
        <div class="cookie-notice__message">
            <svg class="cookie-notice__icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
                <circle cx="11" cy="13.6" r="10.4" fill="#EDA362"/>
                <circle cx="6.73" cy="10.21" r="1.64" fill="#8C5327"/>
                <circle cx="13.3" cy="8.02" r="1.31" fill="#8C5327"/>
                <circle cx="16.37" cy="14.04" r="1.64" fill="#8C5327"/>
                <circle cx="8.7" cy="16.56" r="1.42" fill="#8C5327"/>
                <circle cx="13.63" cy="19.18" r="1.2" fill="#8C5327"/>
                <circle cx="4.54" cy="15.68" r="1.1" fill="#8C5327"/>
                <circle cx="11.44" cy="12.4" r=".93" fill="#8C5327"/>
                <circle cx="20.8" cy="4.2" r="1.6" fill="#EDA362"/>
                <circle cx="22.9" cy="8.6" r=".85" fill="#EDA362"/>
            </svg>

            <p class="cookie-notice__text">
                Cookies keep your search and bookings working. Analytics is
                optional, and one of the two records and replays your session
                &mdash; <a href="/cookies">here&rsquo;s the lot</a>.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="cookie-notice__actions">
            <button type="button" class="cookie-notice__button js-cookie-choice"
                    data-consent="{{ consent.denied }}">No thanks</button>
            <button type="button" class="cookie-notice__button cookie-notice__button--yes js-cookie-choice"
                    data-consent="{{ consent.granted }}">Go ahead</button>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>
